Q = {
'YA-1-1': [
  'A coworker says, “Arguments for God never prove anything, so belief is just a leap in the dark.” Which reply best reflects Catholic teaching?|Reasoning about God is legitimate, but it carries weight only for someone who already believes|The arguments are meant to compel assent, so anyone who resists them is being irrational|Reason can lead to a source of all things, and faith answers the God who reveals himself|Reason can establish that God exists and can also demonstrate the Trinity from creation alone|2|CCC 31-36 says reason can reach God as the source of all, and CCC 50-53 says revelation completes what reason begins.',
  'Why does the Church call natural knowledge of God a “doorway” to revelation rather than a replacement for it?|Reason can reach a first cause, but the Trinity and the Gospel are known only by revelation|Natural knowledge is a rough first draft that revelation later corrects and replaces entirely|Reason is meant to confirm every revealed truth, so nothing is believed that cannot be proven|Revelation is addressed to those whose reasoning about the world has failed to reach God|0|Reason can know that God exists, but what God has made known of himself in Christ lies beyond what reason alone reaches (CCC 50-53).',
],
'YA-1-2': [
  'A friend explains the Trinity as “one God who appears at times as Father, at times as Son, at times as Spirit, like one actor in three masks.” What is the problem?|It teaches three separate gods who happen to cooperate in creating and saving the world|It makes the Son and the Spirit lesser beings who were brought into existence by the Father|It suggests the Trinity can be proven by reason, when it is known only by revelation|It denies that the Persons are really distinct and eternally in relation to one another|3|This is modalism (Sabellius), rejected because the Father, Son and Spirit are really distinct Persons (CCC 253-256).',
  'Why does it matter for ordinary faith that God is a communion of Persons and not one solitary Person?|It shows that God created in order to have someone to love, since love needs an object|Love is then what God eternally is, not something he began to do after creating us|It means three divine beings share the work of salvation and divide it among themselves|It allows believers to treat the Trinity as a puzzle that careful thought will eventually solve|1|“God is love” (1 John 4:8) is true from eternity because the Father, Son and Spirit give themselves to one another (CCC 221, 257).',
],
'YA-1-3': [
  'Someone says, “Jesus was a wonderful moral teacher, but no more.” Which observation most directly challenges that view?|His ethical teaching of love and mercy is far higher than that of any other ancient teacher|He forgave sins and said he and the Father are one, which no mere teacher could claim honestly|He worked miracles, which in the Old Testament always marked someone out as God himself|He was condemned by religious leaders, which shows that his moral message was rejected|1|His hearers understood these claims as claims to divinity (Mark 2:5-7; John 10:30-33), so “teacher only” does not fit the Gospels (CCC 430, 441-445).',
  'Why does Chalcedon insist that Christ has a real human mind and will, and not only a human body?|His divine nature absorbed the human one, so a separate human mind would only duplicate it|A human will kept in perfect harmony with the divine will would make him less than fully divine|Only a human mind could explain why Jesus grew in knowledge and was tempted in the desert|Without a full human nature he could not truly share our humanity and heal it|3|Christ is one divine Person with two complete natures, so he truly shares our humanity (CCC 464-469).',
],
'YA-2-1': [
  'A reader notices that Genesis 1 and modern cosmology describe origins very differently and concludes that Scripture is unreliable. Which is the better Catholic response?|Ask what the author intended in that literary form and time, since Scripture teaches truth for our salvation|Accept that the human authors made scientific errors, because God inspired only the religious message|Defend Genesis as a literal account of the order of events, since inspiration requires scientific accuracy|Treat Genesis as an ancient myth that was edited later and carries no teaching about creation|0|Interpreters must attend to the intention of the human author, his genre and his time (CCC 109-110); the Bible teaches truth for salvation (CCC 107).',
  'Why do Catholics call the human writers “true authors” rather than secretaries taking dictation?|God moved each writer to record his words exactly, so that their personalities added nothing|Their holiness protected them from error, though what they wrote is only human testimony|God worked through their own powers and style, so the words are fully theirs and fully his|The Church later edited their texts so that the finished books would be free of error|2|CCC 106: God chose men who made full use of their own faculties, so that they wrote all and only what he wanted.',
],
'YA-2-2': [
  'A friend says, “If it is not in the Bible, Catholics just made it up and called it Tradition.” What is the best answer?|Tradition is a second collection of teachings that the Church holds alongside the Bible as its equal|Tradition is the set of customs the Church has found helpful and may adjust as culture changes|Tradition is the apostles’ teaching handed on in worship and preaching, from the same source as Scripture|Tradition is the later interpretation of the Bible that the bishops approve in each generation|2|Scripture and Tradition flow from the same divine source and form one deposit of faith (CCC 80-82); Tradition is not a separate set of extra teachings.',
  'Which statement about papal infallibility is accurate?|It guards definitive teaching on faith and morals, but does not make the pope sinless|It means every papal statement, including homilies and interviews, is protected from all error|It allows a pope to define new doctrines, provided that the bishops approve of them afterwards|It preserves a pope from serious sin, so that he is never in need of Confession|0|Infallibility is narrow and serves the deposit of faith (CCC 889-892); it is not impeccability.',
],
'YA-2-3': [
  'A friend says, “I follow Jesus, but I find the Church full of hypocrites, so I will go my own way.” Which reply is most faithful to Catholic teaching?|The charge is overstated, because the Church’s members are kept from serious sin by her holiness|Her sins prove she is a human institution, so Christ’s presence is found elsewhere in prayer|He can keep his faith intact privately, because the sacraments matter less than personal prayer|The Church’s sins are real, yet Christ chose to be present to us through her|3|The Church is holy in Christ yet made of sinners (CCC 827), and Christ gives himself to us through her (CCC 774, 790).',
  'Which of these shows the pattern of Church life seen in Acts 2:42-47?|Private study, personal devotion, solitary fasting and quiet reflection kept away from crowds|Teaching, fellowship, the breaking of the bread and the prayers shared together|Public preaching, charitable works, organized finances and a clear chain of local leadership|Strict moral rules, mutual correction, regular audits and oversight by a single appointed elder|1|The first community devoted itself to the apostles’ teaching, fellowship, the breaking of bread and the prayers (Acts 2:42).',
],
'YA-3-1': [
  'A busy parent says, “My mind wanders constantly when I pray, so I must be failing at it.” Which response is wiser?|Pause until the mind is calm enough that prayer will not be spoiled by wandering thoughts|Distraction is normal and not itself sin; keep returning to God each day|Count each wandering thought as a small failure and confess it before the next prayer|Pray only on days when strong devotion makes it easier to keep attention on God|1|Distraction becomes a problem only if we cling to it; the remedy is to turn back to the Lord (CCC 2729-2733).',
  'Why does the Catechism say prayer is a relationship and not a performance?|Prayer is mainly effective when the person has found words that express the heart well|Prayer is chiefly a discipline for calming the mind, so results are measured by inner quiet|Prayer is a duty we owe God, so its value lies in being completed on schedule|God thirsts for us first, and prayer is our reply to that thirst, an encounter between two thirsts|3|CCC 2560: God thirsts that we may thirst for him; prayer is the encounter of two thirsts.',
],
'YA-3-2': [
  'Someone reads a whole chapter of Scripture quickly each morning but feels nothing. Which change best fits lectio divina?|Read a short passage slowly, linger on a phrase and answer God plainly|Read even more chapters each day, so that the whole Bible is covered within the year|Skip the pause for reflection and move directly to a firm resolution for the day|Study commentaries on the passage until its meaning is settled before attempting to pray|0|Lectio divina aims at meeting a Person, not finishing a text: read, reflect, respond, rest (CCC 2705-2708).',
  'Why does the Church advise testing insights from prayer with Scripture against the Church’s own reading?|Only a priest or scholar can tell whether an insight from prayer is truly from God|Insights gained in prayer are mostly imagination, so they carry no weight on their own|Private impressions can err, and Scripture is read within the Church’s Tradition|The Church must authorize any insight before the person is permitted to act on it|2|Scripture is to be read within the Church’s Tradition and the unity of the whole (CCC 111-114); a wise guide helps test what we hear.',
],
'YA-3-3': [
  'Two Catholics attend the same Sunday Mass. One says, “I just wait for it to be over.” The other says, “I offer my week at the altar with Christ.” What best explains the difference?|The first is not really present, because Mass counts only if the person feels moved by it|The second adds something of his own to Christ’s sacrifice that it would otherwise lack|The second joins his own life to Christ’s offering, which is what praying the Mass really means|They differ in style only, since inner attention changes nothing about what Mass is for|2|We are united to Christ’s one sacrifice and offer ourselves with him (CCC 1368-1369); our faith and attention open us to its fruit (CCC 1128).',
  'On a day when you feel dry and distracted at Mass, what is true?|Christ acts regardless of your mood, though faith opens you to the fruit|The Mass has little effect on you until your own devotion is strong enough to receive it|The sacrifice is complete, but Communion bears fruit only when received with deep feeling|The Mass depends on the priest’s prayer, so your attention affects nothing about its fruit|0|The sacraments work by Christ’s action (ex opere operato, CCC 1128), while the fruit depends on our disposition.',
],
'YA-4-1': [
  'A man says, “I do not feel bad about skipping prayer and Mass for a month, so I cannot have sinned seriously.” What does Catholic teaching suggest?|A sin is grave only when it brings strong guilt at the time that the act is committed|Missing prayer is never sinful, because the Church commands only attendance at Sunday Mass|Only acts that visibly harm another person can be counted as serious sins|Feeling is not the test; gravity, knowledge and consent decide seriousness|3|Mortal sin requires grave matter, full knowledge and deliberate consent together (CCC 1857-1859); how we feel does not settle it.',
  'Why does the Church say sin is more than breaking rules?|Rules exist chiefly to protect the Church’s authority, and sin is disobedience to that authority|It is a failure of love that wounds our relationship with God, with ourselves and with other people|Sin matters only when it harms another person, since offenses against God cannot be measured|Rules are the final standard of holiness, and love adds nothing to what they require|1|CCC 1849: sin is a failure in genuine love for God and neighbor, and wounds the nature of man.',
],
'YA-4-2': [
  'A man who has not been to Confession for twenty years is afraid to go because he cannot remember everything. What is the best advice?|Spend time first reconstructing every sin and its approximate number, then go to Confession|Go, say it has been a long time, and confess what you remember honestly|Ask forgiveness privately in prayer first, and go only after the habits have changed|Confess the recent sins now and leave the older ones for a later and fuller Confession|1|Honest confession of what one remembers is enough, and the priest guides the penitent (CCC 1456, 1483).',
  'A woman is sorry mainly because she fears punishment, not out of love for God. Can she still receive absolution?|No, absolution is valid only when the sorrow springs from pure love of God|No, sorrow rooted in fear shows she has no real intention to change her life|Yes, but only if she first works her sorrow up into love before absolution is given|Yes, imperfect contrition born of fear or shame is still enough for the sacrament to forgive|3|Contrition from fear is imperfect yet still sufficient in the sacrament (CCC 1453).',
],
'YA-4-3': [
  'A woman prays the Our Father daily but refuses to forgive her brother, saying, “God understands.” What does the lesson suggest?|Jesus ties God’s forgiveness to ours, so refusing contradicts her prayer|The petition concerns forgiveness from God, and what she owes her brother is a separate matter|Her forgiveness may wait until he has apologized and tried to make amends for the harm|Forgiveness is fitting for small offenses, but serious wrongs release her from the duty|0|Jesus adds, “If you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father also will forgive you” (Matthew 6:14-15; CCC 2838-2845).',
  'Forgiving a person who abused you most accurately means which of the following?|Forgetting the harm and restoring the relationship to what it was before the abuse|Deciding that the harm was less serious than it felt, so that the anger can end|Letting go of revenge, wishing them well, and still seeking safety|Keeping up contact so that they can see you hold no grudge against them any longer|2|Forgiveness does not require excusing, forgetting or remaining in danger (CCC 2843-2845).',
],
'YA-5-1': [
  'A friend says, “Sacraments are just symbols; the grace comes from my own sincerity.” What is the Catholic response?|They give grace by the rite alone, so a person’s own disposition does not matter at all|They give grace only if the minister is in a state of grace and celebrates them properly|They truly give the grace they signify through Christ, received by faith|They remind us of grace already given, and the effect depends on the person’s sincerity|2|Sacraments work through Christ (ex opere operato), and their fruit depends on our disposition (CCC 1127-1128).',
  'Why does the Church use water, oil, bread and wine in the sacraments?|Since God became flesh in Christ, he continues to give grace through ordinary material signs like these|Ordinary objects are easiest for simple people to understand, so they were chosen for teaching|The early Church adopted common religious symbols of the time to attract new converts|Physical elements are only needed by beginners, and mature believers can receive grace without them|0|Because the Word became flesh, Christ continues to act through visible, material signs (CCC 1115-1116, 1146).',
],
'YA-5-2': [
  'A friend says, “When Jesus said ‘this is my body’ he obviously meant it as a symbol.” Which point best supports the Catholic reading?|Jesus often spoke in figures, and the disciples at the Supper took it as one|The first Christians treated the meal as a simple memorial of the Last Supper|After the Bread of Life discourse, the crowd that heard him stayed to follow him|Paul warns that unworthy reception wrongs the body and blood of the Lord|3|1 Corinthians 11:27-29 treats the eating as more than a symbol; John 6 shows disciples leaving over his words.',
  'What does the Church mean when she says the appearances of bread and wine remain after the consecration?|The bread and wine remain what they were and Christ is present alongside them in faith|The substance becomes Christ’s Body and Blood while the senses still see bread|The elements are changed only while they are being received by the believer in Communion|Careful scientific testing of the elements could detect the change if it were genuine|1|Transubstantiation names a change of substance while the appearances remain (CCC 1376).',
],
'YA-5-3': [
  'A woman baptized as an infant says, “Baptism was just something my parents did.” What does Catholic teaching say?|It begins to take effect only once she personally affirms it as an adult|It made her a child of God for life, a permanent identity that she is now called to live out|It faded during the years of not practicing, so it ought to be repeated now|It was a welcome into the community with no spiritual effect on her soul|1|Baptism imprints an indelible character (CCC 1272-1274); the baptized are called to live as priest, prophet and king.',
  'Which statement about Confirmation is accurate?|It completes religious education, so it is a graduation that marks the end of formation|It gives the Holy Spirit to a person who did not receive him in Baptism at all|It is reserved to adolescents who have been prepared in a school or parish program|It seals the baptized with the Spirit for witness, and adults may receive it|3|Confirmation completes Baptism and strengthens for witness (CCC 1285, 1302-1305); adults may be confirmed.',
],
'YA-6-1': [
  'A friend says, “The Church teaches that the body is shameful, which is why it demands chastity.” What is the better reply?|The body is good and expresses the person, and chastity guards love from using or being used|The body is good, but sexual desire is a weakness that chastity exists to suppress|The body is a temporary vessel, so what we do with it matters less than our intentions|Chastity limits the body’s goodness to marriage, where sexuality finally becomes lawful|0|CCC 2332-2337: chastity integrates sexuality into the person; it protects self-gift and is for everyone.',
  'How can a single person and a married person both be called to live chastity?|Married people are freed from it, because sexual union is permitted to them in marriage|Single people are called to it only until they marry, when the duty then ends|Each lives it in their own state, as self-giving and faithful love|It means avoiding any affection that involves the body, whatever one’s state of life|2|Everyone is called to chastity in their state of life (CCC 2348-2349).',
],
'YA-6-2': [
  'A man has been dating for a year and avoids talking about the future because “it might ruin the mood.” What does the lesson suggest?|Dating is mainly for enjoyment, so serious questions can wait until engagement|Asking about the future signals mistrust, so he should let the relationship develop|Dating is for discernment, so asking where this is going is part of loving well|Prayer will reveal whether this is the right person, without any conversation needed|2|Dating is a time to know whether marriage is possible (CCC 1632, 2350) and requires honesty.',
  'Which of these is a warning sign in a relationship?|One partner controls contact, keeps secrets and pushes with guilt|The partners disagree at times about how to spend their weekends together|The partners agreed on physical limits in advance with the help of a friend|One partner prefers group settings during the early stage of the relationship|0|Control, isolation, pressure and secrecy are red flags; love respects the other’s freedom.',
],
'YA-6-3': [
  'A friend confides that he keeps relapsing into pornography and feels too ashamed to go to Confession. What is the most Christian response?|Urge him to break the habit by willpower first, then go to Confession with a clean record|Tell him the habit is common and not serious, so he need not worry about confessing it|Keep some distance until he shows lasting change, so as not to be drawn in yourself|Reassure him that mercy is meant for exactly this, and encourage him to seek honest help and accountability|3|Shame keeps people hiding; mercy brings them into the light (CCC 1468; Romans 8:1).',
  'Why does the Church call pornography a grave offense?|It stirs up emotions that must be suppressed entirely if purity is to be kept|It uses persons as objects and wounds the dignity of all involved|It displays what the law forbids, so the offense lies in breaking the civil rules|It leads people away from regular church attendance, which harms their spiritual life|1|CCC 2354: pornography offends against chastity because it perverts the conjugal act and gravely offends the dignity of those involved.',
],
'YA-7-1': [
  'A young man delays every major decision, waiting for a clear sign of God’s one right plan. What does the lesson suggest?|Wait until a sign settles the matter, because acting earlier risks stepping outside God’s will|Discern through prayer, self-knowledge and counsel; the greater risk is never choosing at all out of fear|Choose whatever he likes, because God has no preference among good ways of life|Decide on his own in private, since the call comes to each person alone|1|God’s will is a call to love along many faithful paths, discerned over time with counsel (CCC 2690).',
  'What is the first vocation of every Christian, whatever their state of life?|Service in the parish, to which every baptized person is bound by Baptism itself|Family life, which forms the first community in which faith is received and handed on|Ministry in the Church, which belongs in some measure to every baptized believer|Holiness, a life of friendship with God and love of neighbor|3|All the faithful are called to holiness before any particular state (CCC 825, 2013).',
],
'YA-7-2': [
  'A couple plan to marry but privately intend never to have children. How does Catholic teaching see this?|Marriage is ordered to children, so excluding them undermines valid consent|It is acceptable, since the couple’s love for each other is the only essential element|It is acceptable if they receive the sacrament in church before witnesses as required|It becomes a problem only if they decide it after the wedding and not before|0|Consent must include openness to children, fidelity and permanence (CCC 1601, 1625-1632).',
  'What does an annulment declare?|That a valid marriage has ended, so the couple are now free to marry other people|That the children of the marriage are no longer considered legitimate by the Church|That a valid marriage never came into being because of a defect in consent or in form|That the couple’s years together were sinful and have no value in the sight of God|2|An annulment is a finding that the marriage was never valid, not a Catholic divorce (CCC 1629).',
],
'YA-7-3': [
  'A single woman feels her life is “a waiting room” until she marries. Which perspective from the lesson helps most?|It is a temporary phase that ends once she has discovered which vocation is hers|Only those who join a religious order live a complete vocation outside marriage|Single life lived in love and service is a genuine vocation|Waiting itself is the vocation, and she needs not seek to serve in any further way|2|The single life given to God is a genuine way of loving and serving the Church (CCC 897-900).',
  'How does the Church understand the relationship between consecrated life and marriage?|Each is a path to holiness; consecrated life is a special sign|Consecrated life is holier in itself, and marriage is a natural good without grace|Marriage is the higher calling, because it brings new members into the Church|The two are equal and alike, differing only in daily routine and outward practice|0|The Church esteems virginity for the Kingdom while teaching that marriage is a sacrament and a path to holiness (CCC 1619-1620).',
],
'YA-8-1': [
  'A man works eighty hours a week and says, “It is for my family’s future,” though he is rarely home. Which question from the lesson is most helpful?|Is this the highest-paying job in his field that he could realistically take?|Does his employer approve of his level of commitment and long hours?|Is this work respected by the people in his community and extended family?|Does this work serve people, and does it leave real room for God, family and rest?|3|Work is for the person, not the person for work (CCC 2426-2428), and it must leave time for rest and worship.',
  'Why does the Church say work has dignity even when it is humble or tiring?|The wage is fair when all work is paid equally, so no job is lower than another|The worker is made in God’s image and shares in creation by working|Hard labor is a penance that earns merit, which gives it a value beyond its pay|The dignity lies in the product made, since useful goods serve the common good|1|Work’s dignity rests on the worker’s dignity (CCC 2426-2428).',
],
'YA-8-2': [
  'A comfortable family says, “We worked for what we have, so what we give is up to us entirely.” How does Catholic teaching respond?|Property is a gift to be shared, so all of it ought to be held in common ownership|Property is good, but it carries a duty because goods are for all|Giving is generous but optional, since justice concerns only wages and contracts|Only the very wealthy have a duty to the poor, since the rest can scarcely spare it|1|Private property is legitimate yet subordinate to the universal destination of goods (CCC 2402-2406, 2446).',
  'Which is the better approach to personal budgeting in light of the lesson?|Give whatever remains at the end of each month once bills and savings are taken care of|Give only when a serious need appears, since planned giving can become routine|Skip budgeting altogether, since trust in God’s providence replaces careful planning|Make giving to the poor a planned, regular line of the budget, alongside saving and spending|3|Almsgiving is part of discipleship and justice (CCC 2443-2447); regular habits build generosity.',
],
'YA-8-3': [
  'A town council wants to decide how a small local school is run. Which principle suggests the decision be left to the local community?|Subsidiarity, which holds that decisions belong at the lowest competent level|Solidarity, which holds that every person is responsible for the good of all others|The common good, which holds that all affected parties must share in every decision|The preferential option for the poor, which holds that the least advantaged decide|0|Subsidiarity keeps decisions close to those affected (CCC 1883-1885).',
  'What is the best description of the Church’s role in politics according to the lesson?|She endorses the party closest to the Gospel and expects her members to support it|She stays out of public life and limits herself to the spiritual needs of believers|She teaches principles and forms consciences, but does not endorse a party|She directs members how to vote on each issue, since the principles are not in doubt|2|Catholic social teaching challenges every ideology; applying principles is left to prudence (CCC 2420, 2442).',
],
'YA-9-1': [
  'A friend whose child is very ill asks, “How can a good God allow this?” What is the wisest first response?|Explain that God permits suffering because it always brings about a greater good|Assure her that God has a reason for it, so she should trust that it will be made clear|Be present and listen to her pain, rather than offering a tidy explanation for the suffering|Remind her gently that suffering entered the world through sin and shares in its effects|2|Job receives God’s presence, not an argument; the first response to a sufferer is presence.',
  'Which statement about redemptive suffering is consistent with Catholic teaching?|It can bear fruit with Christ, but is never sought or used to excuse injustice|It should be sought out deliberately, since pain itself pleases God and earns merit|It has no meaning, so endurance is only a matter of will and good character|It means that abuse should be accepted quietly as part of God’s plan for the person|0|Sufferings joined to Christ’s can be redemptive (Colossians 1:24; CCC 1521); Christians must also relieve suffering and protest evil.',
],
'YA-9-2': [
  'A student worries that learning about the Big Bang and evolution will cost him his faith. What is the best advice?|Reject any scientific claim that seems to conflict with Genesis, since faith has priority|Treat Genesis as a myth with no truth to teach, since science explains origins now|Keep the two apart in his mind, because they answer questions that never overlap|One God authors both, so sound science cannot truly contradict faith|3|CCC 159: there can never be any real discrepancy between faith and reason.',
  'Which claim goes beyond what science can show and is a philosophical position?|The universe has a history that spans many billions of years in its development|Only what can be measured and tested is true|Living species have developed and changed over very long periods of time|Natural events can be studied through methods that are tested and repeated|1|Scientism is a philosophical position, not a scientific finding; the Church welcomes sound science (CCC 159, 283-284).',
],
'YA-9-3': [
  'A Catholic says, “Since the Church respects other religions, it does not really matter what anyone believes.” What is the right response?|That is right, because the Church teaches that all religions lead to the same salvation|The Church respects what is true in them but proclaims Christ as sole Savior|The Church rejects all that other religions teach, since only her doctrine is true|Respect means avoiding the subject of Christ in dialogue so as not to give offense|1|Nostra Aetate 2 and CCC 843-846: the Church rejects nothing true and holy, yet proclaims Christ.',
  'What best describes the Catholic approach to dialogue with other Christians?|Treat other Christians as outside the Church’s concern, since they are not in communion|Play down doctrinal differences so that agreement can be reached more quickly|Avoid joint prayer and shared work in order to protect the faith from confusion|Seek unity through prayer and honest conversation, honoring the real bond that Baptism creates|3|Other baptized Christians are in imperfect communion with the Church (CCC 818-819), and unity was Christ’s prayer.',
],
'YA-10-1': [
  'A man says, “I pray and read Scripture, so I do not need a community or a mentor.” What does the lesson suggest?|Faith is lived with others who know us, challenge us and keep us faithful|He is right, since private prayer and Scripture reading are the center of the faith|He needs a community only in a season of doubt or after a moral failure|He should join any group that welcomes him, since all communities help equally|0|We are made for communion, and the Church is where faith grows (Hebrews 10:24-25; CCC 1878-1880).',
  'Which is a warning sign of an unhealthy community?|It asks members to commit a set amount of time every week to its meetings|It holds members to a clear standard of belief and conduct that is publicly stated|It discourages questions and contact with family, and demands secrecy|It sometimes challenges members to change habits that are harming their relationships|2|Authority serves, and the fruits of healthy community are freedom, truth and charity.',
],
'YA-10-2': [
  'A woman wants to share her faith with a coworker who is not religious. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Prepare strong arguments and keep making them until the coworker has to concede|Wait until she has mastered theology, so that she is not asked something she cannot answer|Befriend and listen, live so that questions arise, then invite|Leave faith materials where the coworker will find them, so that no pressure is felt|2|Evangelization starts with witness and friendship and is always an invitation, never a trap (1 Peter 3:15-16; CCC 160).',
  'Why is it wrong to pressure someone into faith?|Faith is a free response to God, so it can be invited and witnessed to but cannot be forced|Pressure rarely works, while gentle persuasion over time brings in many more people|Inviting others belongs to clergy and missionaries, and not to lay members of the Church|People ought to be left to find faith on their own, without any word from believers|0|CCC 160: to be human, man’s response to God by faith must be free.',
],
'YA-10-3': [
  'A man makes a rule of an hour of prayer, daily Mass, fasting and weekly service, and quits within two weeks. What does the lesson advise?|Set the rule even higher, so that quitting becomes harder to justify to himself|Drop the idea of a rule, since grace alone sustains a life of faith, not human plans|Keep the full rule and treat each lapse as a sin to be confessed before going on|Start small, add one habit at a time, and begin again without drama|3|A rule is a trellis for grace, not perfection; start small and review it with a friend or director.',
  'What is the danger of legalism in a rule of life?|The rule grows so flexible that nothing in particular is ever actually asked of the person|The rule becomes the goal and replaces loving God and neighbor|The rule leads others to see you as holier than they are, which feeds pride in you|The rule demands help and structure that busy people do not realistically have time for|1|The goal is love, not perfection; the rule serves grace.',
],
'YA-11-1': [
  'A friend says, “If the pope is infallible, he cannot sin or be wrong about anything.” What is the correct response?|He is correct, since the pope is preserved from error in every statement he makes|It is narrow, covering only definitive teaching on faith and morals, and not every statement|It applies to papal decisions on Church discipline and administration as well|It is an honorary title and does not guarantee any teaching in particular|1|Infallibility covers definitive teaching on faith and morals (CCC 889-892), not impeccability or opinion.',
  'Which is an example of authentic development of doctrine, as opposed to change?|The Church reverses an earlier moral teaching once the culture has shifted decisively|The Church adds a revealed truth that was unknown to the apostles themselves|The Church quietly drops a teaching that has become hard for people to accept|The Church states more clearly what was always believed about Christ|3|Doctrine develops in understanding without changing in meaning (CCC 66, 94); Nicaea clarified, not invented.',
],
'YA-11-2': [
  'A student says, “The Church has always been right, so those stories about slavery must be exaggerated.” What does the lesson suggest?|Members of the Church sinned gravely, yet popes and saints spoke against it|The Church has done only harm, and nothing good can be said of her record|A few individuals sinned, but the Church’s official teaching never tolerated slavery|The past is irrelevant, because the Gospel itself stands apart from any history|0|The Church is holy in Christ yet always in need of purification (CCC 827); truth includes both failure and witness.',
  'What does the Church teach about responsibility for the death of Jesus?|The Jewish people of that time bear a shared responsibility that passes to later generations|Only the Roman authorities are to be held accountable, since they carried out the execution|Sinners are responsible, and Jews are not collectively guilty|No one bears any responsibility, since the Crucifixion was part of God’s plan|2|CCC 597-598 and Nostra Aetate teach that guilt cannot be imputed to the Jewish people as a whole.',
],
'YA-11-3': [
  'A Protestant friend says, “Asking Mary to pray for you takes away from Jesus.” Which reply fits Catholic teaching best?|Mary is a second mediator who adds to what Christ has accomplished for us|Mary is worshiped as God is, because she is his mother and shares his dignity|Mary’s intercession shares in his mediation, like any friend’s prayer|Asking for her prayers is optional and lacks any foundation in Scripture or practice|2|Honor given to Mary differs from the worship due God alone (CCC 971, 2096); her role leads to Christ (CCC 969).',
  'What does the Immaculate Conception teach?|Mary was kept free of original sin from her conception, by Christ’s grace|Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit without a human father in Mary’s womb|Mary was taken body and soul into heaven when her life on earth was ended|Mary remained a virgin before, during and after the birth of Jesus her Son|0|The Immaculate Conception concerns Mary’s own conception (CCC 490-493); it is not about the virginal conception of Jesus or the Assumption.',
],
'YA-12-1': [
  'A Catholic finds a Church teaching hard and says, “My conscience tells me it is fine to ignore it.” Which response is most faithful?|Follow conscience as it stands, since a person must always obey it however it was reached|Obey the teaching outwardly and set the doubts aside, since feelings should not guide|Conclude that the teaching is outdated, because it conflicts with the sense of what is right|Conscience must be formed, so he should study the teaching honestly, pray and ask a wise guide first|3|We must obey a certain conscience, yet we are responsible for forming it (CCC 1783-1785, 2039).',
  'When is a Christian justified in refusing to obey a civil authority?|Whenever a law is costly or inconvenient, because conscience outranks every civil rule|When it commands something morally evil, since God comes first|Whenever the person disagrees with the policy behind the law and has voted against it|Only in times of persecution, since the state’s authority comes from God in other cases|1|Acts 5:29 and CCC 2242: citizens are obliged in conscience not to follow directives contrary to the moral order.',
],
'YA-12-2': [
  'A voter says, “Since no party matches Church teaching perfectly, a Catholic should not vote.” What does the lesson suggest?|He is right, since no Catholic can take part in politics without compromising the faith|Participation is a duty; form conscience on all the principles|Catholics should support the party that the bishops have formally endorsed in their country|Catholics should vote on a single decisive issue and treat the others as secondary matters|1|Participation is a duty of citizenship (CCC 2240), and the Church teaches principles without endorsing a party.',
  'Which attitude toward fellow Catholics who vote differently fits the lesson?|Assume that those who vote differently have set aside the Church’s teaching on conscience|Avoid politics in conversation with them, so that your own convictions are not weakened|Ask them to reconsider their vote before sharing a table or serving with them in the parish|Differ in good faith on means, avoid contempt and keep friendship|3|Catholics may differ on prudential applications; contempt violates charity (CCC 2477-2479).',
],
'YA-12-3': [
  'A man who prayed fervently for years now finds prayer empty and says, “God has left me.” Which perspective fits Catholic teaching?|Dryness is common, and fidelity in prayer through it can deepen love|Dryness shows his faith has faded, so he should stop praying until it returns|Dryness is a punishment for neglect, which will last until he repents of it|Dryness means he has outgrown set prayer and should seek a new method|0|Teresa of Calcutta and John of the Cross show faith as fidelity, not feeling (CCC 2726-2728).',
  'A woman has been persistently hopeless and sad for months and calls it a dark night. What does the lesson advise?|Treat it as a spiritual trial and endure it in prayer, since medical help cannot reach it|Take it as a sign of weak faith, and answer it with more prayer and more discipline|Seek a doctor or counselor as well, since dryness and depression differ|Wait for it to pass before telling anyone, since the dark night is a private matter|2|Faith and healthcare are partners; hopelessness warrants medical help.',
],
'YA-13-1': [
  'A parishioner regularly arrives at Mass in time for the offertory, saying the readings are just a warm-up. What is wrong with this?|Nothing is wrong, since only the Eucharistic part of the Mass is required of the faithful|Missing the readings is a problem only when the homily is also missed, as it explains them|In the readings Christ speaks to his people; both halves are one worship|The first half is preparation, and it matters mainly for the priest and the readers|2|The two parts of the Mass form one act of worship (CCC 1346-1349).',
  'Why does the Creed follow the homily?|It is the people’s response, professing what they have heard and believe|It summarizes the homily, so that the main points of the sermon are recalled|It tests the faith of anyone who wishes to go on to receive Communion|It takes the place of the psalm, which answers the first reading in the same way|0|The Creed is the assembly’s answer to the Word proclaimed (CCC 1349).',
],
'YA-13-2': [
  'A visitor says, “At Mass the priest is just reenacting the Last Supper as a drama.” What does the Church teach?|It repeats the sacrifice, so that Christ is offered up again at every Mass|It is a memorial that helps people recall the Supper, with no sacrifice involved|It adds the Church’s offering to the Cross, which Christ left to be completed|It makes Christ’s one sacrifice present, so it is real and not a drama|3|The sacrifice of the Cross and the Eucharist are one sacrifice (CCC 1364-1367).',
  'Why does the Church kneel or bow at the consecration?|Christ is present only in the faith of the assembly, which deserves respect and reverence|Christ is truly present, body, blood, soul and divinity, under the appearances of bread and wine|The priest acts by his own power at this moment, and the people honor his office|The gestures are old customs of the liturgy that carry no particular doctrinal meaning|1|CCC 1374-1378: the Eucharist is adored because Christ is really present.',
],
'YA-13-3': [
  'A man is aware of a mortal sin he has not confessed but joins the Communion line to avoid attracting attention. What does the Church teach?|He should receive, since God sees his heart and the Eucharist itself heals the sin|He should go to Confession first, as Communion requires grace|He should receive, because staying in the pew would cause scandal for those nearby|He should wait and receive during the Easter season, which satisfies his obligation|1|CCC 1385, 1415: anyone aware of mortal sin must receive the sacrament of Reconciliation before Communion.',
  'What does the dismissal “Go in peace” invite Catholics to do?|Leave the church quietly, so that others may remain in prayer and thanksgiving|Carry the Eucharist to the sick and homebound as the Mass comes to its end|Rest from work for the remainder of the day and reflect on the homily|Live what they have received as the Body of Christ in the world|3|The Eucharist commits us to the poor and sends us on mission (CCC 1397).',
],
'YA-14-1': [
  'A man with a heart condition is awaiting major surgery and thinks the Anointing of the Sick is only for the dying. What is the right answer?|He may, since it is for serious illness and before surgery, and it can be repeated if he worsens|He may not, because it is reserved to those who are at the point of death|He may receive it once in his life, so he should wait until his illness is worse|He may receive it from a deacon if no priest is available before the operation|0|CCC 1514-1515: the sacrament is for serious illness or before surgery, and it can be repeated.',
  'What does the Church promise about the effects of the Anointing of the Sick?|Full bodily healing for the person, provided that enough faith is present|Forgiveness of sins only, without any consolation for the body or the spirit|Strength, peace and union with Christ’s Passion, and healing if God wills|A sure recovery from the illness, so long as the person receives it in time|2|CCC 1520-1523: grace of strength and peace, forgiveness of sin, and healing when God wills.',
],
'YA-14-2': [
  'A man says, “I will live as I like and sort it out in the next life.” Which teaching most directly answers him?|After death a person is given a further chance to choose God in the light of truth|Everyone is saved in the end, so the manner of one’s life changes only the delay|Each person is judged at death, so choices made in this life matter|Judgment waits until the end of the world, when all will be judged together|2|CCC 1021-1022: each person receives eternal retribution in the particular judgment at death; there is no reincarnation (CCC 1013).',
  'What best describes the Church’s teaching about hell?|It is real: a freely chosen, definitive exclusion from communion with God|It is a symbol for the pain of this life, and no one actually experiences it|It is a punishment God designs for those he never intended to save|It is a state the Church has confirmed for particular named individuals|0|CCC 1033-1037: hell is self-exclusion, God wills all to be saved, and no individual has been declared damned.',
],
'YA-14-3': [
  'A woman asks, “If my father is in purgatory, am I wasting my time praying for him?” What does the Church say?|Yes, since his state was settled at death and the living cannot change it|No, though only the saints in heaven are able to help souls in purgatory|Yes, since souls in purgatory are already assured of heaven and need no help|No, Mass, prayer and mercy help the dead through the communion of saints|3|CCC 1032, 1371: the Church offers prayer, almsgiving and above all the Eucharist for the dead.',
  'Which statement about purgatory is accurate?|A further chance for those who rejected God during their life to repent after death|A final purification of the saved, who die in God’s friendship but are not yet ready to see him|A temporary hell where the unrepentant suffer until they are released at the end|A neutral waiting place where souls remain unchanged until the last day arrives|1|CCC 1030-1031: those who die in God’s friendship but imperfectly purified are assured of salvation.',
],
'YA-15-1': [
  'A reader is disturbed by violent passages in the Old Testament and says the God of the Old Testament is not the God of Jesus. Which response fits the lesson?|Set those passages aside, as Marcion proposed, since they do not reflect the true God|God teaches gradually; read these passages in the light of Christ|Treat each such passage as a direct command that still binds believers in our own day|Read them as symbols only, since the events they describe were never historical|1|CCC 53, 122: God’s teaching is gradual; Marcion’s rejection of the Old Testament was condemned.',
  'Which sense of Scripture comes first, and why?|The allegorical, since it connects every text to Christ and gives the others their point|The moral, since the Bible is given chiefly so that we may learn how to act|The anagogical, since all Scripture is aimed at the final goal of eternal life|The literal sense, since the spiritual senses all rest on what the human author meant to say|3|CCC 116: all other senses of Scripture are based on the literal.',
],
'YA-15-2': [
  'A reader notices that the Gospels tell some events differently and concludes they are unreliable. What does the lesson suggest?|Differences are expected from different witnesses, with the same Jesus|The differences show that some of the accounts were invented by later communities|The accounts need to be harmonized into one text before they can be called accurate|The differences matter to scholars only and have no bearing on what believers read|0|The Gospels were formed in three stages and written with different purposes (CCC 126).',
  'Why does the Church keep four Gospels rather than one?|Later councils kept four so as to settle disputes between communities that preferred one|Each Gospel was written to correct the errors that the earlier accounts had made|Each shows Jesus for its own audience, giving a fuller picture|A single account could not have preserved the words of Jesus without distortion|2|Each Evangelist selected and arranged material faithfully, giving a fuller portrait (CCC 126).',
],
'YA-15-3': [
  'A man says, “I cannot pray the Psalms, because some express anger and hatred, and that cannot be prayer.” What is the best reply?|Skip those Psalms, since anger and vengeance have no place in prayer to a God of love|Treat them as permission to answer wrongs in kind, since Scripture records them|Bring the anger honestly to God rather than act on it|Pray only the Psalms of praise and thanksgiving, which the Church reserves for worship|2|The Psalms teach honesty with God; the Church reads them in Christ (CCC 2589, 2597).',
  'Why do Christians pray the Psalms in the light of Christ?|Jesus prayed them, and the Church reads them as fulfilled in him|They were composed by the apostles after the Resurrection to express the new faith|They became Christian prayer only when Greek hymns were added in the early centuries|The Church set them aside long ago in favor of newer prayers and hymns of her own|0|Jesus prayed the Psalms, from the Last Supper to the Cross (CCC 2587).',
],
'YA-16-1': [
  'A man says, “My conscience is clear, so I did nothing wrong,” though he never learned the Church’s teaching on the matter. What does the lesson suggest?|A clear conscience always excuses, since a person cannot be blamed for what he sincerely believed|Conscience has no authority of its own and must simply follow what the Church says|Honest ignorance never excuses, so the act counts against him in just the same way|A clear conscience is not enough; ignorance excuses only if not his fault|3|Ignorance excuses only if it is invincible, and we have a duty to form conscience (CCC 1790-1793).',
  'Which is the best way to form conscience?|Follow the opinion most people in one’s culture and time hold on the question|Scripture, Church teaching, prayer, examination and wise counsel|Follow personal feelings after a period of quiet reflection and prayer|Follow one trusted friend’s advice in every moral matter, without testing it|1|CCC 1783-1785: conscience is formed by the Word of God, prayer, examination and good counsel.',
],
'YA-16-2': [
  'A man who wants to become more patient asks how virtue grows. Which advice fits the lesson?|Pray for patience and wait, since virtues are gifts that effort cannot produce|Small repeated acts, with grace and good models, one virtue at a time|Rely on strong will and work at several virtues together so that they support each other|Avoid any situation where patience would be tested until the habit is firm|1|Virtues are built by repeated acts with grace, as grace perfects nature (CCC 1810-1811).',
  'Why can we not give ourselves the theological virtues?|They need a natural talent that only some people are born with, and others lack|They are given to the clergy and the consecrated, and others share them by association|They are built by habit like the other virtues, though it takes much longer to do so|Faith, hope and charity are infused by God and directed to him, so they are received, not earned|3|CCC 1812-1813: the theological virtues are infused by God at Baptism.',
],
'YA-16-3': [
  'A student says, “The Ten Commandments are a list of restrictions that keep life from being enjoyed.” Which reply best reflects the lesson?|God first freed Israel, then gave the law as the shape of life in freedom|God gave the law first so that Israel could earn its freedom by keeping it faithfully|The commandments belong to the ancient world and Christ replaced them with feelings|They are ideals to aim at, which Jesus made optional by stressing love above rules|0|The Decalogue follows the Exodus (Exodus 20:2; CCC 2057-2063).',
  'How do the Beatitudes relate to the Commandments?|The Beatitudes replace the Commandments with a gentler set of rules for Christians|The Beatitudes are counsels for the clergy, and the Commandments are for the laity|The Commandments mark love’s minimum; the Beatitudes show its full height|The Commandments describe the goal of the Christian life, and the Beatitudes the means|2|Jesus came not to abolish the law but to fulfill it (Matthew 5:17; CCC 1716-1729).',
],
'YA-L1': [
  'A manager says, “I will rest once this project is done,” and has said it for three years. Which insight from the lesson speaks to him?|Rest is a reward for work well done, so he is right to put it off until the work ends|Rest matters chiefly because it restores energy, so he should nap and return to the work|Rest is commanded, not earned, and it says that he is not what he produces|Rest is a private matter of personal health that has no bearing on his faith|2|Sabbath rest is grounded in creation and in freedom from slavery (CCC 345-348, 2168-2172, 2184-2185).',
  'Why does the Church place Sunday Mass at the center of Sunday rest?|We stop working so as to receive something from God at Mass, and not merely to recover|Mass fills the day with an obligation, so that no one is tempted to go back to work|Mass is a rule the Church added later to keep believers active in parish life|Attendance mainly gives families a regular time to see friends and relatives each week|0|Sunday is the Lord’s day, the first day of the new creation (CCC 2174-2176, 2184-2185).',
],
'YA-L2': [
  'A woman realizes she spends hours each day scrolling and then feels restless and envious. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Make one strong resolution to stop and trust that the willpower will carry her through|Accept the restlessness as an ordinary feature of modern life and not worry about it|Delete every app and avoid all technology from now on, so temptation is removed|Choose deliberately what gets her attention, as the heart follows the eyes|3|Purity of heart is a long work of attention and desire, with grace and practice (CCC 2517-2527).',
  'According to the lesson, what is the Church’s remedy for envy and endless wanting?|Strict self-denial of every pleasure, so that desire is gradually silenced altogether|Poverty of heart and a desire to see God, which reorder what we long for|A firm budget that limits purchases, so that wanting has less to feed on|Ignoring the achievements of others, so that comparison has nothing to start from|1|CCC 2544-2548: poverty of heart and the desire for God order our wanting.',
],
'YA-L3': [
  'A couple earn more each year but never feel they have enough. Which question from the lesson helps them most?|How can we raise our income more quickly, so that we can finally feel secure?|What is enough for us, and what is the rest for?|How can we show others that we have earned what we own through honest work?|How can we avoid all risk with what we have saved, so that none of it is lost?|1|Without an answer to “enough,” no amount is enough (CCC 2536, 2547); wealth is to be used for others.',
  'Which statement holds together the two principles of private property and the universal destination of goods?|Owning property is good, so the owner decides everything about how it is to be used|Everything belongs to everyone, so private ownership is unjust and should be ended|Property is morally neutral, so ethics has nothing to say about how it is used|Property is good but held for the common good, so the needy have a claim|3|CCC 2402-2406: private property is legitimate but subordinate to the universal destination of goods.',
],
'YA-L4': [
  'A man is angry at a friend who betrayed him and thinks, “My anger proves I am a bad Christian.” What does the Catechism teach?|Anger is not itself sinful, but it becomes sin when it wills harm or hardens into hatred|All anger is sinful, so he ought to suppress it completely and quickly|Anger is sinful only if he expresses it aloud to the other person involved|Anger is acceptable so long as he was the one wronged and it is a just cause|0|CCC 2302-2303: anger is a passion that is sinful only when it desires revenge or is disordered.',
  'A woman says, “I have forgiven him, but I do not feel it, and I still do not want to see him.” How does the lesson see her situation?|She has not yet forgiven, because forgiveness is not real until the feelings change|She has not yet forgiven, because forgiveness requires meeting and being reconciled|Forgiveness is a decision, and does not require trust or reconciliation|She has forgiven only if she can honestly say that the harm was not serious|2|CCC 2843 says we cannot control feelings, but the heart offered to the Spirit turns injury into intercession.',
],
'YA-L5': [
  'A coworker tells you, accurately, that another employee was fired for misconduct. You pass it on to a friend who has no connection to the matter. What is this?|Calumny, because you spread a story that harmed the employee’s reputation|Rash judgment, because you assumed a fault without sufficient foundation|Detraction: the fault was true but revealed to someone with no right to it|Nothing wrong, since every word you said was true and was told to you|2|CCC 2477: detraction discloses another’s faults to those who did not know them, without a valid reason.',
  'What does the Catechism advise before concluding that someone meant harm?|Interpret their words favorably where possible, and ask what they meant|Compare their behavior with others who act in similar ways, and judge by the pattern|Wait until a second person confirms the suspicion, so that it can be trusted|Assume the worst so as not to be taken advantage of by someone who may mean harm|0|CCC 2478: avoid rash judgment by interpreting the neighbor’s words and deeds in a favorable way.',
],
'YA-L6': [
  'A widow living alone says, “The Church is for families, so there is no place for me.” Which response best reflects the lesson?|Her situation is a private matter outside parish life, and the parish cannot help|She should find a family of her own by moving closer to relatives or remarrying|Only relatives can fill the need she describes, so the Church can offer little|The Church is family for those alone too, through parish and saints|3|The communion of saints and the domestic church speak to loneliness (CCC 946-962, 1655-1658).',
  'According to the lesson, what is loneliness?|A sign of weak faith that more prayer alone will cure in time|A sign of a real need for communion, not a personal failure|A passing mood that fades once one is kept busy enough with activity|A result of having chosen a single or celibate way of life|1|We are made for communion in the image of the Trinity (CCC 1878-1879, 221).',
],
'YA-L7': [
  'Visiting a friend who is dying, you feel pressed to say something comforting. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Assure him that God sent this for a reason that will become clear in time|Be present and listen, without explaining the suffering away or insisting that he be cheerful|Encourage him to stay positive, since a hopeful outlook helps him bear the illness|Keep to happy memories and avoid the subject of dying, which only distresses him|1|The Church does not explain suffering away; Christ is present in the sick (Matthew 25:36; CCC 1503-1505).',
  'What does the Church mean by saying suffering can be “not wasted”?|It is God’s way of testing us, and it always earns merit in proportion to its weight|It teaches life lessons that improve the character of the one who suffers it|It is deserved, so enduring it repays a debt that we owe because of sin|Joined to Christ’s, it can bear fruit, though we do not seek it|3|CCC 1505, 1521: sufferings joined to Christ’s Passion share in his redemptive work.',
],
'YA-L8': [
  'A woman must decide whether to move for a job, but cannot tell what God wants. Which approach best fits the lesson?|Form conscience, seek counsel, pray, weigh prudently, then decide|Wait for a clear sign from God before deciding, since acting earlier is presumption|Follow her strongest feeling, since a decision felt deeply is how God makes it known|Choose the option most people around her recommend, since they know her best|0|Prudence takes counsel, judges and decides (CCC 1806); God works with imperfect choices.',
  'Which rule applies even when a case is unclear?|Choose the option that brings about the greatest total benefit for the most people|Choose the option that carries the least personal risk for herself and her family|Never do evil so that good may result|Choose the option that her conscience finds the most comfortable at the time|2|CCC 1789: one may never do evil that good may come of it.',
],
'YA-L9': [
  'A man in his fifties avoids thinking about his death. Which approach does the lesson recommend?|Avoid dwelling on it, since thoughts of death lead to anxiety and unhappiness|Keep death before him as a source of fear, since fear makes him live more carefully|Remember death so that it clarifies what matters, and prepare for it in practical and spiritual ways|Leave all preparation to the family, to be dealt with when the time actually comes|2|The Church asks us to remember death so as to be ready to meet Christ (CCC 1013-1014).',
  'Why does the Church offer Mass and prayers for the dead?|Many die in God’s friendship yet need purification, which prayer helps|Prayers can change the verdict of the judgment that the dead have already received|Prayers are needed to bring souls out of hell, since the Church’s mercy reaches there|Prayers help the dead avoid the final resurrection, which the faithful are said to fear|0|CCC 1030-1032: prayer for the dead is an ancient practice tied to purification.',
],
}
